NEWS (Claude summaries, facts only)
- CNC [positive] (cached): Centene (CNC) reached a 52-week high on November 5, 2026, according to a Zacks article that asks whether the stock has more room to run. Several same-day commentaries tie managed care and healthcare stocks to shifting policy expectations after the midterm elections, and Cigna beat Q3 estimates.
  Drivers: Stock at 52-week high on Nov 5; Midterm election shifts in policy expectations; Cigna Q3 beat and raised 2026 EPS outlook; Peer Humana expecting higher Q3 premiums
- MRK [neutral] (cached): Few headlines name Merck directly. The main MRK-related item is Halozyme reporting a Dutch patent victory against Merck, while US litigation between the two continues. Separately, global healthcare stocks fell on Friday in a broader market rout.
  Drivers: Halozyme Dutch patent win against Merck; US patent litigation still ongoing; Broader healthcare sector sell-off
- MRNA [neutral] (cached): Moderna shares rose about 13% on Wednesday to a new 52-week high after early multiple myeloma data and a WHO technical advisory group backing its Ebola vaccine candidate mRNA-1469 for a Phase 2/3 study, then fell about 5% on Thursday. The company is scheduled to report fiscal Q3 earnings on Nov. 6, and Barchart reports that options data suggests shares could fall after the update.
  Drivers: WHO advisory backing for mRNA-1469 Ebola vaccine candidate; Early multiple myeloma data; Fiscal Q3 earnings scheduled for Nov. 6; Options data implying possible post-earnings decline
- TXN [neutral] (cached): Texas Instruments priced $2.0 billion of senior unsecured notes to help fund its planned acquisition of Silicon Laboratories and cover transaction costs, according to a Simply Wall St. article that also reports a 5.63% 30-day share price return. Headlines also report that ETFs bought $144.6 million of TXN on Nov. 3, and that the Avantis U.S. Large Cap Value fund added $49.7 million of TXN that day. An Investing.com piece says the stock is up 76% over a year while AI chip stocks have stumbled.
  Drivers: Planned Silicon Laboratories acquisition and $2.0B debt financing; ETF and fund buying in early November; Strong 1-year share price gain; Broader AI chip sector weakness
