  MRNA [neutral]: Moderna shares rose about 13% on Wednesday to a new 52-week high after early multiple myeloma data and a WHO technical advisory group backing its Ebola vaccine candidate mRNA-1469 for a Phase 2/3 study, then fell about 5% on Thursday. The company is scheduled to report fiscal Q3 earnings on Nov. 6, and Barchart reports that options data suggests shares could fall after the update.
  SNDK [negative]: SanDisk shares fell about 20% on November 5 as an AI-related sell-off deepened, even though the company reported strong earnings. The decline tracked Nvidia and Broadcom, with investors questioning the outlook for AI data center expansion. The Nasdaq entered correction territory the same day.
  LITE [negative]: Marvell agreed on November 5 to buy Lumentum in a $115 billion all-stock deal, and Lumentum shares fell about 13% to 15% that day amid a broad AI and optical-stock selloff. TIKR reports that Lumentum trades on Marvell's share price because Marvell is paying in stock without price protection.
  MU [negative]: Micron stock fell 17% on Thursday, November 5, as a broad chip-sector sell-off led by AI-trade stocks pushed the Nasdaq into a correction. Headlines tie the selling to post-midterm AI regulation fears and new data-center restrictions, and one headline describes Micron's valuation multiples as unusually low after the decline.
  DELL [negative]: Dell shares fell sharply on November 5 as a broad tech and AI-infrastructure selloff deepened after the midterm elections, with one report citing an 11% drop and no company-specific news. Coverage also noted that analysts expect Dell's earnings to more than double this fiscal year, and that its forward P/E is near its five-year average.
  SPCX [negative]: SpaceX (SPCX) shares fell on November 5 and extended declines into November 6, as Wall Street weighed possible regulatory hurdles after the Democratic midterm sweep. Reports also said AT&T and T-Mobile have discussed removing Boost Mobile from their networks if SpaceX acquires the EchoStar unit, and the broader market sell-off spread beyond AI stocks.
  PLTR [negative]: Palantir reported 96% revenue growth, its fastest in its public company history, yet the stock still fell as investors sold off tech and AI names amid a broader sell-off. As of Nov 6 it traded at $161.69, up 4.32% intraday, though one outlet describes the daily trend as still weak.

